                <pb facs="0433" n="405" sameAs="frus:frus1961-63v05/pg405"/>

                <head>181. Editorial Note</head>
                <p>During 1961 and 1962, Kennedy
                    administration officials became increasingly concerned about a possible arms
                    race in outer space. On September 25, 1961, for instance, President Kennedy proposed in an address <pb facs="0434"
                        n="406" xml:id="pg406"/> before the UN
                    General Assembly “keeping nuclear weapons from seeding new battlegrounds in
                    outer space” and extending the rule of law on earth “to manʼs new domain—outer
                    space.” (<hi rendition="#italic">Documents on Disarmament, 1961</hi>, pages
                    470-471) The U.S. treaty outline on general and comprehensive disarmament,
                    submitted to the Eighteen-Nation Disarmament Committee on April 18, 1962,
                    proposed that the parties to the treaty “agree not to place in orbit weapons
                    capable of producing mass destruction.” (Ibid., 1962, volume I, page 360) For
                    information on subsequent measures concerning a possible arms race with the
                    Soviet Union in outer space and relevant documentation, see <ref
                        target="frus:frus1961-63v07/pg_563"><hi rendition="#italic">Foreign
                            Relations</hi>, 1961–1963, volume VII, pages 563</ref>–<ref
                        target="frus:frus1961-63v07/pg_565">565</ref>.</p>
            </div>
